1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:18,000
This is the result: a relevant product inside Twitch, visibly approved by the creator, with viewer interest shown only after a safe cohort forms.

2
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:38,000
StreamCart combines creator picks with live Channel3 discovery. Every recommendation explains where it came from, and viewers can open the real product or the full catalogue.

3
00:00:38,000 --> 00:01:02,000
The creator steers the AI from Twitch chat. A signed EventSub webhook accepts this command only from that broadcaster.

4
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:22,000
The AI proposes; the creator decides. Approval changes the provenance in place, and the API binds that write to Twitch's signed channel claim.

5
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Viewer intent is explicit. StreamCart stores no viewer ID, profile, or browsing history—only channel, product, action, and aggregate count.

6
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Channel3 supplies the live catalogue. StreamCart turns returned brands into instant filters while keeping the same safety policy and visible provenance.

7
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Counts stay hidden below five and are then bucketed. A single viewer cannot be inferred from an exact low number.

8
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Codex took over the vertical slice, verified the Twitch and Channel3 contracts, built the signed authority and privacy boundaries, and retained release evidence for this session.

9
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:45,000
The live Twitch subscription is enabled, Channel3 returns real products, and CI covers builds, eleven API tests, twenty-two listing checks, and the dependency audit.

10
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:55,000
StreamCart: creator-controlled discovery, useful AI, and privacy by design—inside Twitch.
